WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.120
Previous works have shown us that fire&nbsp;
simulation is possible. You can set a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.680
virtual tree on fire and see what happens.&nbsp;
Or, on a bigger scale, simulating wildfires!&nbsp;

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:17.200
But what about extinguishing the&nbsp;
fire? Not like this. With water!

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.840
Well, I found an amazing research&nbsp;
work on that too! Yoohoo!

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.800
Now, there are different types of flames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.720
depending on what kind of chemical created&nbsp;
them. This incredible work models that too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.000
that is already stunning. But it’s&nbsp;
nothing compared to what it can do.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.680
So the main problem is that fire simulations&nbsp;
are like the ones in video games. It’s like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.440
plastic display burger in a restaurant&nbsp;
ad. It looks fine, until you touch it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.800
or if you throw a bucket of water on&nbsp;
it, nothing happens - the water just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.720
clips right through it! This problem goes&nbsp;
way beyond games, such a thing could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.800
amazing for fire safety training too! You could&nbsp;
actually try to train firefighters by putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.280
out realistic fires in VR. But you can’t do&nbsp;
that if the fire is ignoring the water hose!

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.360
So, how do we fix this? Well, this new research&nbsp;
work says just give it the geometry of the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.440
a fuel source, and a water source. And it&nbsp;
promises a chemically rigorous simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.720
where the fire actually dies if you&nbsp;
starve it of oxygen or cool it down.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.800
Here is the crazy thing. You can even mix&nbsp;
different fuel types and fuel-oxygen ratios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.360
and they create completely different kinds of&nbsp;
flames. How an Earth did they do that? Wow.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:48.800
And when we hit the fire with water, we finally&nbsp;
get some vapor! So cool. It’s really tough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:55.360
put out though, look, even if you aim at&nbsp;
the base… it didn’t do a great deal. Why?

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:02.480
Because a laminar flow is essentially a solid&nbsp;
beam of water with minimal surface area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.240
which limits its ability to&nbsp;
absorb heat from the flames.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:14.960
Now let’s try a proper spray instead. Does this&nbsp;
work better? Oh my, you bet it does! Why exactly?

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.320
This works beautifully because breaking&nbsp;
the water into thousands of tiny droplets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:27.280
increases the surface area for heat absorption.&nbsp;
This cools the area down instantly while, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.960
the huge expanding steam suffocates the fire.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.400
Now this is a virtual world, so we&nbsp;
can try funny things. You often hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.760
the term adding fuel to the fire. So&nbsp;
why not do that? Oh wow, look at that!

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.400
Now note that the visuals are not the&nbsp;
state of the art. I need to mention that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.160
simulation and visualization are different&nbsp;
disciplines. The true treasure here is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.520
accurate chemistry simulated under&nbsp;
the hood, and not the pretty pixels.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:07.120
Now it advects the fire, and also starts scorching&nbsp;
this wall next to it. Look closely at how the wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.040
darkens over time - this is not just some&nbsp;
pre-computed color being slapped on it! The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.440
simulation actually tracks the formation of soot&nbsp;
during incomplete combustion and even deposits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:27.360
them onto the object's surface. It simulates the&nbsp;
environment having a memory of being burned. Wow.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.320
Now, real life firefighting gets so much&nbsp;
more intricate than these toy examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:39.920
so let’s put it to the real test. Can it simulate&nbsp;
this? Okay, wait, so what is happening here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:47.360
This is a brilliant application of the Venturi&nbsp;
effect. They are spraying water not into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:55.280
window. But out of the window, that is the key.&nbsp;
Why? Well, if you do that at a high speed, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.280
lower the air pressure there, which essentially&nbsp;
vacuums the smoke and heat out of the room.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.000
It is like a massive truck speeding&nbsp;
down a highway. This creates a gust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.200
of wind that pulls dry leaves behind it.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.640
The simulator cannot possibly be so&nbsp;
smart to understand this right? Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.920
see…I can’t believe my eyes. This is incredible!

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.920
Now one other detail that completely blew&nbsp;
me away is the annealing simulation. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.760
they heat up a metal rod, and when the&nbsp;
flame is removed, the rod stays glowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.360
and slowly cools down. It even creates&nbsp;
its own light source! This wasn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.200
the main point of the paper, but it adds&nbsp;
so much realism. I absolutely love this.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.560
Okay, now, the two final boss scenes,&nbsp;
and then I’ll tell you how it works.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:56.400
Boss level 1. Three cars burning with massive&nbsp;
flames. Yes! Finally! This puts it all together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:02.000
This is a multiphase experiment. This is a&nbsp;
fancy way of saying that the Holy Trinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.680
of physics states are all fighting each other:&nbsp;
solids, liquids and gases. All at the same time.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.840
You see that this is not just deleting the&nbsp;
fire. The liquid water hits the hot gas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.600
absorbs the heat, and transforms into white&nbsp;
steam that mixes with the black smoke.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.440
It is a chaotic, beautiful mess of&nbsp;
thermodynamics! The water fights the fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.160
the fire fights the air, and&nbsp;
the steam fights for space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.560
The simulation is calculating the chemistry&nbsp;
of extinction. And now hold on to your papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:45.440
Fellow Scholars, because all this happens&nbsp;
in real-time. Yup. I am out of words.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.440
Now let’s demonstrate how this&nbsp;
kind of knowledge can save lives.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.440
Here we have a kitchen fire starting on a&nbsp;
stove. We activate the sprinkler with just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:03.680
a tiny bit of delay. A tiny bit. And look. Oh&nbsp;
goodness! The fire grows, climbs the walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.160
eats the ceiling, and fills the whole room with&nbsp;
thick, black smoke. It is a total disaster.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.880
Now a simulation technique like this can help us&nbsp;
imagine a what if scenario. What if we activated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.720
the sprinkler just a bit earlier? Probably&nbsp;
doesn’t matter right? Well, hold on to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:28.720
papers Fellow Scholars, yes, again, and look at&nbsp;
the difference! In the second timeline, the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:36.880
spray cools the reaction instantly. You see the&nbsp;
fire turn into a puff of white vapor and die out.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:43.440
This proves why this isn't just a toy for&nbsp;
making video games look cool. No! This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:49.040
a virtual safety lab. Don’t forget,&nbsp;
this is accurate and fast. With this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.080
we can test millions of what if scenarios&nbsp;
- different sprinkler positions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:07:00.800
different delays and fuels. All this without&nbsp;
ever having to burn down a single house.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.240
Okay, so how is all this magic possible?&nbsp;
How did they do that? Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.200
this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:16.320
Well, first of all, this doesn’t use any AI&nbsp;
whatsoever. Only human brilliance. Okay. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.720
reason previous techniques failed is that fire&nbsp;
and water in a computer speak two completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.320
different languages. The fire lives on&nbsp;
a grid - imagine a giant 3D spreadsheet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:35.120
of boxes that calculates airflow and&nbsp;
temperature. But the water…the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:41.840
lives as particles - like millions of tiny sand&nbsp;
grains floating freely. In older simulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:47.600
these two worlds couldn't talk to each other&nbsp;
fast enough, so the water particles would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:54.400
fly through the fire grid like ghosts. To fix&nbsp;
this, these amazing scientists built a high-speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:01.840
translator that sits between these two worlds&nbsp;
and forces them to interact. That is brilliant.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.760
Okay, so why is that important? Well, here&nbsp;
comes the magic. Because of this new translator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:13.600
the water and fire can finally speak to&nbsp;
each other. When a droplet hits a hot spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.400
it demands some heat from the fire,&nbsp;
and because they are connected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:24.240
the fire has to give it up. The water&nbsp;
uses that stolen heat to turn into steam.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:30.320
This creates a chain reaction where the fire&nbsp;
cools down, and the new steam crowds the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.880
pushing the oxygen away to suffocate the&nbsp;
flames. And now all of these speak the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.960
language. The water can finally extinguish&nbsp;
the fire instead of just passing through it.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:49.440
But that’s not all. Not even close. Under the&nbsp;
hood, the simulation uses a famous mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:56.240
formula called the Arrhenius equation to control&nbsp;
the fire. Think of this as the fire's gas pedal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.400
It calculates exactly how fast the fuel should&nbsp;
burn based on the current heat and the available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:08.960
oxygen. Because this equation is super sensitive&nbsp;
to temperature, even a small splash of water that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:15.360
lowers the heat causes the math to slam on the&nbsp;
brakes, instantly stopping the chemical reaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:21.760
So, the fire physically stops burning&nbsp;
simply because the math says it is too cold.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.560
Now, surprisingly, there is so much more to learn&nbsp;
here. Not just about simulation techniques, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:36.800
about life itself. You see, the simulation proves&nbsp;
that a solid beam of water fails, but a spray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:43.760
succeeds because it maximizes contact area. That&nbsp;
is great life advice! Sometimes you don’t need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:51.040
solve a crisis with one huge heroic effort. Break&nbsp;
the solution down into many tiny droplets. Tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:57.520
little tasks. You might find that they absorb&nbsp;
the heat of the problem much better. So good.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:04.560
Also, remember, the paper simulates the kitchen&nbsp;
disaster before it happens. You can do that too!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.680
Imagine yourself in the future and imagine&nbsp;
that your friendships, marriage or career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:16.480
has already failed. Now the question is, if&nbsp;
it failed, why did it fail? Work backwards to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:22.880
find the cause. Then, like the sprinkler, you&nbsp;
can fix the problem before it even happens.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.080
Okay, with all that said, let’s not&nbsp;
overstate things. Yup, not even this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:34.000
technique is perfect. The authors note that&nbsp;
the solids in the simulation are static. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.600
geometry has to be fixed, so that’s why you&nbsp;
are seeing simulations with lots of metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:46.480
and not elastic trees burning. I think that&nbsp;
is an acceptable tradeoff. But of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:53.200
here we invoke the First Law of Papers, which says&nbsp;
that research is a process. Do not look at where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:59.760
we are, look at where we will be two more papers&nbsp;
down the line. And in two more papers, baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

